# Tutorial testing

This directory contains the tutorial end-to-end test suite. It extracts the
```shell code blocks from the published tutorial pages and runs them, in
order, inside a clean VM via [Spread](https://github.com/canonical/spread).

The goals are:

- catch documentation drift (commands that no longer work as written),
- catch product regressions that break the documented happy path,
- do so without duplicating content: the tutorial Markdown is the single
  source of truth.

## Prerequisites

- [Multipass](https://multipass.run/) (the test VM backend)
- [Spread](https://github.com/canonical/spread): `go install github.com/canonical/spread/cmd/spread@latest`
- Python 3.10+ (stdlib only; no pip packages needed)
- Make

## How it works

1. `extract_commands.py` scans the tutorial Markdown pages and, for each
   page containing a `<!-- test:spread ... -->` metadata block, generates:
   - `<page>.sh` — the extracted shell script,
   - `<page>/task.yaml` — the Spread task definition.
2. Only ` ```shell ` fenced blocks are extracted. Any other fence tag
   (`bash`, `text`, `json`, ...) is ignored — this is the primary mechanism
   for marking sample *output* as non-executable.
3. HTML comment annotations placed before/between blocks control the
   generated script: waits, assertions, retries, hidden commands, etc.
   See the "Annotations" section of the STAT README for the full reference.
4. Spread runs the generated tasks in a fresh Multipass VM, ordered by the
   `priority` field in each page's metadata block (higher runs earlier).

## Adding a new tutorial page

1. Add a `<!-- test:spread ... -->` metadata block to the page:

   ```
   <!-- test:spread
   priority: 500
   kill-timeout: 60m
   -->
   ```

   Choose `priority` to slot the page into the right position in the run
   (leave gaps of ~100 between pages for future insertions).

2. If you maintain the `SCRIPTS` list in the `Makefile` (for incremental
   `make all` builds), add the new `output.sh:source.md` pair there too.
   `make extract` and tox use directory discovery and need no updating.

3. Regenerate and sanity-check:

   ```shell
   make extract
   bash -n tasks/<page>.sh   # generated script must parse
   ```

4. Run the suite locally (see below) before pushing.

## Run modes

From this directory (or via `make -f tests/tutorial/Makefile` from the root):

| Command | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `make extract` | Regenerate all scripts (directory discovery) |
| `make all` | Regenerate only changed pages (via `SCRIPTS`) |
| `make check` | Verify generated files are up to date; no writes |
| `make test` | Regenerate, then run the suite; abort on first failure |
| `make test-continue` | Run all tasks even if some fail |
| `make test-debug` | Drop into an interactive shell in the VM on failure |
| `make clean` | Remove generated scripts and task directories |

Via tox (from the project root):

```shell
tox -e tutorial-extract   # generate only
tox -e tutorial           # generate + run the suite
tox -e tutorial-check     # CI guard: fail if generated files are stale
```

## Debugging a failed run

- `make test-debug` opens a shell in the VM at the exact point of failure.
- When a task fails, the suite's `debug-each` script runs
  `collect_diagnostics` (from `helpers.sh`), dumping `juju status`,
  `juju debug-log`, machine info and host state into
  `$SPREAD_PATH/tasks/artifacts/<system>/` inside the VM (the exact path
  is printed in the task output). Run Spread with `-artifacts=./artifacts`
  to fetch the directory locally; each job's artifacts land under
  `./artifacts/<backend>:<system>:<suite>/<task>/`.
- `spread -vv` prints all task output; without it, only failures show.

## CI behaviour

The `Tutorial tests` workflow (`.github/workflows/tutorial-tests.yaml`)
runs the suite:

- monthly on a schedule, plus via manual `workflow_dispatch`,
- on a self-hosted runner with KVM (Multipass requires it),
- with a 3-hour job timeout and an `if: always()` Multipass VM purge.

The workflow is intentionally *not* run on every PR: a full tutorial run
takes 1-3 hours. `tox -e tutorial-check` in the regular CI pipeline guards
against stale generated files instead.

## Maintenance notes

- Generated files (`tasks/*.sh`, `tasks/*/task.yaml`) are gitignored;
  regenerate them locally, never hand-edit.
- Environment variables set in one page's script do not persist to the next
  page (each page is a separate script/process). Persist state on disk
  inside the VM instead.
- Keep assertions deliberately loose (e.g. `grep -q` for a key field rather
  than full-output comparison): strict assertions fail on harmless
  formatting changes and create false negatives.
